<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Popular Front (France)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Front populaire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> was an alliance of the French Socialist, Radical and Communist parties, formed against fascism, which won the elections of 1936. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-blum">Léon Blum</a>, the first socialist prime minister, its government introduced the 40-hour week, two weeks' paid holiday and collective bargaining through the Matignon Agreements. Divided over non-intervention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> and hit by economic difficulties, it broke up by 1938.</p></div>
